# EDL-0008: Expand the Relational Data Model for Scheduling and Participation

- Status: Under review
- Date: 2026-11-09
- Decision owners: Geopestejunior and BDeha
- Related repositories:
  - Target application repository: [event-volunteer-hub](https://github.com/Community-TOS-Projects/event-volunteer-hub)
- Related Issue:
  - [event-volunteer-hub#28](https://github.com/Community-TOS-Projects/event-volunteer-hub/issues/28)
- Related Pull Request:
  - [event-volunteer-hub#29](https://github.com/Community-TOS-Projects/event-volunteer-hub/pull/29)
- Related EDL entries:
  - EDL-0001: Select MeetHub as the Initial Inherited Codebase
  - EDL-0006: Establish Event Volunteer Hub Project Identity and Licensing
  - EDL-0007: Adopt Proposal v2.0 as the Event Volunteer Hub Project Direction

- Affected areas: Event, Shift, Attendance, SignUp, Organization, account compatibility, migrations, tests, and Django administration

## Context

Issue #28 proposed extending the inherited Event Volunteer Hub event structure to support volunteer scheduling. The inherited MeetHub implementation provided a single event time field and no mechanism for representing event duration, volunteer shifts, organizational hosting, or attendance tracking.

PR27 and PR39 later clarified that RSVP, Sign-Up, Assignment, and Attendance are separate concepts with different responsibilities and lifecycle rules. The initial PR29 implementation did not fully separate those concepts: `Shift.volunteers` functioned as an implicit assignment mechanism, and `Attendance` stored workflow-like statuses in addition to participation information.

The model redesign was therefore extended beyond the original scheduling work. The implementation in scope is limited to architecture and data-model changes. Frontend interfaces, endpoint workflows, coordinator approval screens, notifications, organization ownership, and waitlists remain follow-up work.

## Decision

Expand the relational data model to support event scheduling, volunteer shifts, sign-ups, confirmed assignments, attendance tracking, organizations, capacity, and distinct participation concepts.

### Event scheduling

- Replace `Event.time` with `Event.start_time` and `Event.end_time`.
- Order events by `date`, `start_time`, and `end_time`.
- Preserve the existing event time through the `0014` migration.
- Replace the legacy `num_of_attendees` field with `capacity` using `RenameField` in migration `0015`.
- Add positive event capacity validation in the model.
- Treat legacy `capacity=0` as the existing meaning for now. A separate future decision will define how to backfill or migrate unset legacy capacity values.
- Add `signup_mode` with direct and approval choices for future workflows.

### Volunteer shifts

- Add a `Shift` model with `name`, `event`, `start_time`, `end_time`, `description`, and `capacity`.
- Associate shifts with events through `event = ForeignKey(Event, related_name='shifts')`.
- Replace `Shift.volunteers` with Sign-Up records.
- Require shifts to start before they end and remain within the event time range.
- Reject overlapping shifts for the same event.
- Reject shifts that cross midnight.
- Enforce model validation on save with `full_clean()`.

### Sign-Up and assignment

- Add a `SignUp` model with `user`, `event`, optional `shift`, `status`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.
- Use `pending`, `confirmed`, `declined`, and `cancelled` as the stored status values.
- Pending and confirmed Sign-Ups reserve capacity; declined and cancelled Sign-Ups remain history and release capacity.
- Support Event-level reserve Sign-Ups with no selected Shift.
- Use a confirmed Sign-Up as the Assignment record. Do not add a separate Assignment model initially.
- When a reserve is assigned to a Shift, update the existing Sign-Up's optional Shift relation instead of creating a duplicate record.
- Prevent duplicate active Shift Sign-Ups and duplicate active Event-level reserves through conditional uniqueness constraints.

### RSVP and event capacity

- Keep `Event.attendees` as the separate RSVP relationship.
- Count the distinct union of RSVP attendees and active Sign-Up users for Event participation.
- Count pending and confirmed Sign-Ups as active capacity users.
- Count each person once toward Event capacity even if they RSVP or Sign-Up for multiple Shifts.
- Count Shift capacity using distinct pending or confirmed Sign-Ups assigned to that Shift.

### Organizations

- Add an `Organization` model with `name`, `description`, `website`, `contact_email`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.
- Do not add direct relationships to accounts or events in this work; organization ownership remains follow-up work.

### Attendance

- Retain an `Attendance` model with `user`, `event`, optional `shift`, `status`, `hours_recorded`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.
- Store only attendance states: `attended` and `absent`.
- Remove sign-up and approval states from Attendance.
- Require an Attendance Shift, when present, to belong to the same Event.
- Preserve uniqueness for each `(user, shift)` pair.

### Account compatibility and administration

- Use the custom `accounts.Account` model and email-based identity rather than Django's default User assumptions or a `username` field.
- Update affected account lookups, displays, factories, and tests to use valid Account fields and `get_fullname()`.
- Register Organization and Shift administration and update Event and Attendance administration for the revised fields.

### Migration

The implementation includes migrations:

- `0014_alter_event_options_rename_time_event_start_time_and_more`
- `0015_remove_event_num_of_attendees_and_more`
- `0016_alter_attendance_status_alter_event_capacity`

Migration `0014` renames `Event.time` to `Event.start_time`, adds `Event.end_time`, renames `Shift.max_volunteers` to `capacity`, adds `Shift.name`, and updates Event ordering.

Migration `0015` renames `num_of_attendees` to `capacity`, removes `Shift.volunteers`, adds `signup_mode`, changes Attendance to attendance-only choices, and creates `SignUp` with active Sign-Up uniqueness constraints.

Migration `0016` aligns the final Attendance status choices and Event capacity field state.

The existing migrations do not automatically migrate historical `Shift.volunteers` rows into Sign-Up records or transform historical Attendance workflow records into Sign-Up records. These remain documented follow-up work rather than silent data transformations.

## Rationale

The original Event structure could not represent event duration, volunteer shifts, sign-up lifecycle states, or separate attendance and assignment records. Separate Event start and end times complete the schedule, while Shifts model volunteer work periods within that schedule.

RSVP remains the Event attendee relationship, Sign-Up represents a volunteer request or reserve, a confirmed Sign-Up represents assignment, and Attendance represents recorded attendance and hours. Keeping these concepts separate prevents workflow status fields from being mixed into RSVP or attendance records.

This data-model foundation preserves existing Event timing data and existing architecture while avoiding UI, coordinator, notification, organization ownership, and task-workflow assumptions that have not been implemented.

## Alternatives Considered

### Keep a single Event time field

Rejected. A single time cannot represent Event duration or support shift scheduling.

### Keep `Shift.volunteers` and `Attendance` as workflow structures

Rejected. `Shift.volunteers` is an implicit assignment mechanism, while Attendance workflow statuses conflate requests and actual attendance.

### Add a separate Assignment model now

Rejected for the initial implementation. A confirmed Sign-Up can serve as the Assignment record, with the optional Shift updated for reserve placement.

### Keep legacy `num_of_attendees=0` meaning unchanged

Rejected as a future positive-capacity design. This contribution leaves the existing value and backfill behavior unchanged; a later decision will address legacy unset capacity separately.

### Automatically migrate legacy Attendance workflow rows

Rejected for this contribution. Different environments may contain sign-up states with nonzero hours, and silently changing or discarding those values would risk data loss.

### Continue using username-based lookups

Rejected. The custom Account model uses email identity and `get_fullname()`; Account-compatible lookups are consistent with its authentication architecture.

## Consequences

### Positive

- Events represent complete schedules.
- RSVP, Sign-Up, confirmed Assignment, and Attendance have separate model responsibilities.
- Pending and confirmed Sign-Ups can reserve Event and Shift capacity through model logic.
- Shift and attendance validation improves data integrity.
- Existing Event timing data is preserved through migration.
- Custom Account compatibility is addressed in tests, forms, views, and administration.
- Automated tests cover Event, Shift, Attendance, forms, views, and RSVP workflows.

### Tradeoffs

- The participation schema is more complex and requires future workflow and migration handling.
- Legacy Shift and Attendance data is not automatically transformed.
- Legacy capacity values and backfill remain unresolved.
- Organization ownership, coordinator approval, notifications, waitlists, and Position/Task workflows remain outside this contribution.
- `signup_mode` exists in the schema but is not exposed in user-facing interfaces.

## Evidence

Validation included Django model tests, migration verification, view tests, form tests, Shift validation testing, RSVP attendance testing, Django Admin review, and Event scheduling testing.

Specific checks include:

- Event forms and templates use `start_time` and `end_time`.
- Event tests use the custom Account model and capacity values.
- Attendance tests verify attendance-specific statuses and Shift/Event relationship validation.
- Shift tests verify containment, non-overlap, adjacent boundaries, and cross-midnight rejection.
- Deletion tests verify that deleting an Event removes its associated Shifts and Attendance records and deleting an Account removes its Attendance records.
- RSVP tests verify attend and leave behavior, duplicate attendance, anonymous-user redirects, nonexistent Events, and Event detail redirects.
- Factory definitions use `capacity`, current time fields, and DjangoModelFactory compatibility.

The Events test suite can be run with:

```text
python manage.py test meethub.events
```

## Follow-Up Work

- Backfill or otherwise resolve legacy Event capacity values in all relevant environments.
- Decide and implement a data-preserving migration for legacy `Shift.volunteers` records.
- Decide and implement a safe migration for legacy Attendance workflow records.
- Add frontend RSVP and Sign-Up interfaces.
- Add direct and approval-based Sign-Up endpoints and capacity checks.
- Add reserve placement and cancellation workflows.
- Add coordinator approval interfaces.
- Decide organization-to-account and organization-to-event relationships.
- Consider waitlists, Position, and Task assignment workflows.
- Consider shift-overlap, overnight scheduling, and Attendance consistency rules in later work.
